{
  "image_name": "f4bc7b920a79cc97e80416fda1578c14.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several physical and chemical mechanisms that generate colour in nature. At the top, it contrasts pigment-based colour, bioluminescence, and structural colour. Pigments produce colour by selectively absorbing some wavelengths and reflecting others, while bioluminescence is shown as a chemical reaction involving luciferin, luciferase, and oxygen that emits photons directly. Structural colour instead comes from nanoscale architectures that redirect light through reflection and scattering rather than chemical absorption.\n\nThe lower panels expand the main optical strategies behind structural colour. Thin-film interference is illustrated for both a single layer and multilayer stacks, where light reflects at interfaces and the path differences determine the observed colour. Diffraction gratings split incident light into multiple coloured directions, indicating wavelength-dependent spreading. Photonic crystals are shown in one-, two-, and three-dimensional periodic arrangements, emphasizing that ordered structures can control how light propagates. Cholesteric liquid crystals are depicted as helically organized layers with different pitches, reflecting different colours. Metasurfaces appear as arrays of tiny surface features that steer reflected light in designed directions.\n\nOverall, the image shows that biological and material colours can arise from either chemistry or precisely organized micro- and nanoscale structure.",
